    "result_text": "I implemented `complete_paragraph` in `app/tgi.py` and the tests pass. It creates `InferenceClient(model=endpoint_url)`, the same way `app/titles.py` does, and calls `text_generation` with:\n- `max_new_tokens=200`\n- `stop=[\"\\n\\n\"]`: in the pinned huggingface_hub 2.0.0 this argument is called `stop`. The older name, `stop_sequences`, no longer exists in this version and would raise a `TypeError`.\n- `return_full_text=False`, so the prompt is left out of the result.\n\nIt then returns the result with `.rstrip()`. TGI keeps the stop sequence at the end of its output, so this also removes the trailing `\"\\n\\n\"`. Leading whitespace is kept (e.g. `\" world.\"`), since you only asked for trailing whitespace to be removed.\n\n**Testing:**\n- `pytest -q`: 1 passed. That test only checks that the module imports, so it doesn't cover the new code.\n- I also ran a short script with the HTTP call mocked. It showed the request goes to the endpoint URL with `{'max_new_tokens': 200, 'return_full_text': False, 'stop': ['\\n\\n']}`, and that a server reply of `\" world.\\n\\n\"` comes back as `' world.'`. I didn't run it against a real TGI server.\n\n**One cleanup for you:** I wasn't allowed to delete the scratch script, so `_check_tgi.py` is still in the project root and you can delete it. I didn't add or change any dependencies."
